WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 47: The Bloody Benders: The Devil's Inn on the Prairie

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.520
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.800
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:17.600
[Rebecca] In the early 1870s, southeastern Kansas was a bustling, sun-baked thoroughfare for pioneers, cattlemen, and prospectors traveling southwest along the Osage Trail.

4
00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:26.640
[Rebecca] Dotted along the lonely dirt tracks were small wooden shanties where travelers could water their horses, purchase a hot meal, and find a bed for the night.

5
00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:36.560
[Rebecca] But along an isolated stretch of prairie near Cherivale, Kansas, stood a modest wooden cabin operated by a sinister German spiritualist family, the Benders.

6
00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:46.240
[Rebecca] Posing as humble innkeepers, old John Bender, his fierce wife, and their attractive, charismatic daughter Kate welcomed wealthy travelers into their parlor.

7
00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:51.360
[Rebecca] But behind a decorative canvas wagon sheet curtain, a sledgehammer was waiting.

8
00:00:51.360 --> 00:01:03.040
[Rebecca] In 1873, the gruesome discovery of an apple orchard filled with butchered corpses horrified the nation, creating one of the most chilling, unsolved mysteries of the American West.

9
00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:07.840
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story.

10
00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:20.640
[George] In the autumn of 1871, four German immigrants took up a hundred and sixty-acre homestead claim in La Bette County, Kansas, 14 miles northeast of the frontier railroad town of independence.

11
00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:29.840
[George] The family consisted of 60-year-old John Bender, a hulking, sullen man who spoke very little English and was nicknamed Old Man Bender.

12
00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:42.160
[George] His 60-year-old wife Elvira, a fierce, foul-tempered woman rumored to be a witch, their 27-year-old son John J.R., an eccentric youth prone to wild, inappropriate laughter.

13
00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:44.960
[George] And their 23 year old daughter Kate.

14
00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:47.200
[George] Kate was the star of the family.

15
00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:58.960
[George] She was striking, fashionable, and well spoken, advertising herself as a spiritualist healer, clairvoyant, and psychic medium who could cure blindness and communicate with the spirits of the dead.

16
00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:15.320
[George] Realizing that their homestead sat directly along the heavily traveled Osage Trail, the main route connecting Fort Scott to the Texas border, the Benders divided their modest 24 by 16-foot wooden cabin into two rooms using a heavy canvas wagon sheet curtain.

17
00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:25.320
[George] The front room served as a general store, grocery and dining room where weary stagecoach travelers could buy cheese, crackers, tobacco and hot meals.

18
00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:28.920
[George] The back room served as the family's private living quarters.

19
00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:40.440
[George] The Osage Trail was a lonely, windswept wagon road in Labette County where homesteaders and travelers journeyed west without telegraph stations or civil law enforcement for dozens of miles.

20
00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:48.520
[George] Beginning in 1872, an ominous and baffling pattern of disappearances began along the roads of southeastern Kansas.

21
00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:59.320
[George] Solitary horsemen, cattle buyers carrying rolls of bank currency, and entire families traveling westward vanished into thin air between Fort Scott and Independence.

22
00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:08.920
[George] Empty wagons with their teams of horses wandering aimlessly were found abandoned along the roadside, but of the drivers, not a single trace could be found.

23
00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:17.000
[George] Local newspapers began warning travelers that a ruthless gang of horse thieves or murderous outlaws was stalking the lonely trails.

24
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:20.520
[George] At first, no one suspected the Bender family.

25
00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:30.440
[George] Travelers regularly stopped at the little grocery for lunch, where pretty Kate Bender entertained diners with palm readings and philosophical discussions on spiritualism.

26
00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:35.960
[George] But a few travelers who escaped the cabin later recalled chilling, bizarre encounters.

27
00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:42.120
[George] One man remembered sitting at the dining table and noticing the heavy canvas curtain twitching behind his head.

28
00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:52.720
[George] Catching a glimpse of old man Bender standing in the shadows wielding a heavy iron sledgehammer, prompting the guest to leap from his chair and run for his life through the prairie grass.

29
00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:59.600
[George] The Bender cabin was divided into two rooms by a heavy wagon canvas sheet, behind which John Bender Sr.

30
00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:00.480
[George] and John Jr.

31
00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:04.800
[George] waited with ten-pound sledgehammers while guests ate meals at the table.

32
00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:10.320
[George] The sinister mystery finally unraveled in the spring of 1873 with the disappearance of Dr.

33
00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:11.920
[George] William Henry York.

34
00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:12.400
[George] Dr.

35
00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:18.800
[George] York was a wealthy, prominent physician from Fort Scott who had traveled to Montgomery County to visit his brother.

36
00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:22.800
[George] On his return journey home in March 1873, Dr.

37
00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:28.320
[George] York paused at the Bender Cabin for dinner and a horse feed, and was never seen alive again.

38
00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:31.120
[George] What the Benders did not know was that Dr.

39
00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:37.120
[George] York belonged to one of the most politically powerful and influential families in the state of Kansas.

40
00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:47.680
[George] One brother, Alexander York, was a distinguished member of the Kansas State Senate, while another brother, Colonel Ed York, was a decorated Civil War cavalry commander.

41
00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:48.960
[George] When Dr.

42
00:04:48.960 --> 00:05:01.520
[George] York failed to return to Fort Scott, Colonel York mobilized a search party of fifty heavily armed former cavalrymen and detectives, retracing his brother's trail mile by mile across the prairie.

43
00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:09.040
[George] Questioning every homesteader along the route until the trail led inexorably to the doorstep of the Bender Cabin, when Dr.

44
00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:20.800
[George] William York, a respected physician and brother of Kansas State Senator Alexander York, vanished after stopping at the inn, a massive armed search party rode across the county.

45
00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:26.560
[George] In late April 1873, Colonel York and his men rode up to the Bender Cabin.

46
00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:33.960
[George] Old Man Bender and his wife acted hostile and claimed they knew nothing, but young Kate stepped forward with poise and charm.

47
00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:35.560
[George] Kate admitted that Dr.

48
00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:41.960
[George] York had indeed stopped at their cabin for lunch, claiming he had ridden off safely toward the east.

49
00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:52.520
[George] Kate offered to use her clairvoyant spiritual powers to hold a seance and contact the spirits to locate the missing doctor, inviting Colonel York to return on Friday night alone.

50
00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:57.720
[George] York politely accepted, but his instincts told him something was terribly wrong.

51
00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:08.120
[George] Days later, a massive township meeting was held at the Harmony Schoolhouse, where seventy local homesteaders agreed to systematically search every cellar and barn in the township.

52
00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:10.440
[George] Both old man Bender and John J.

53
00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:10.840
[George] R.

54
00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:14.600
[George] attended the meeting, listening quietly in the shadows.

55
00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:18.920
[George] The following morning, a violent spring rainstorm struck the county.

56
00:06:18.920 --> 00:06:28.120
[George] When neighbors rode past the Bender homestead several days later, they noticed that the horses were starving in their pens and the farm was completely silent.

57
00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:32.200
[George] The cabin was abandoned, the Benders had fled into the night.

58
00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:38.120
[George] Senator York personally questioned Kate Bender, who claimed psychic powers and offered to divine Dr.

59
00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:43.240
[George] York's whereabouts, while secretly preparing the family wagon for a midnight escape.

60
00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:53.080
[George] When township searchers and Colonel York forced open the wooden door of the abandoned Bender cabin, they were hit by a sickening, overpowering stench of putrefaction.

61
00:06:53.080 --> 00:07:01.880
[George] Pushing aside the heavy canvas curtain, searchers discovered a secret trapdoor cut into the wooden floorboards, fastened with a leather strap.

62
00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:06.280
[George] Lifting the trapdoor revealed a six-foot-deep cellar beneath the dining room.

63
00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:13.320
[George] The dirt walls and floor were soaked in pools of clotted, dried human blood, but no bodies were inside.

64
00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:23.040
[George] Outside, searchers noticed that the soft soil of the small family vegetable garden and young apple orchard was marked by sunken, rectangular depressions.

65
00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:27.440
[George] Armed with shovels and metal probing rods, the men began digging.

66
00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:30.400
[George] What they uncovered horrified the entire world.

67
00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:35.520
[George] Buried head down in shallow graves were twelve brutally mutilated human corpses.

68
00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:35.920
[George] Dr.

69
00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:41.360
[George] William York was the first to be uncovered, followed by eleven other missing travelers.

70
00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:50.000
[George] Among the victims was a young father, George Lonker, and his 18-month-old daughter, who had been buried alive with her murdered father.

71
00:07:50.000 --> 00:08:00.160
[George] Every adult victim had been killed in the exact same mechanical fashion, their skulls crushed from behind with a sledgehammer, their throats slashed from ear to ear.

72
00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:10.880
[George] Searchers smelled the stench of decay coming from the garden orchard, where digging revealed eleven graves containing the bludgeoned and slit-throat bodies of missing travelers and a young child.

73
00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:18.480
[George] As detectives reconstructed the crime scene, the terrifying, industrial efficiency of the bender trap became clear.

74
00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:28.720
[George] When a guest entered the cabin to eat, Kate Bender seated the traveler in the guest chair of honor, placed deliberately with its back pressed against the heavy canvas wagon curtain.

75
00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:37.840
[George] While Kate engaged the victim in lively conversation from across the table, one of the bender men, usually the towering old man Bender or John J.

76
00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:42.240
[George] R., crept silently behind the canvas curtain in his stocking feet.

77
00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:50.480
[George] Striking with immense force through the cloth, the killer smashed an eight-pound iron blacksmith's sledgehammer into the back of the victim's skull.

78
00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:54.720
[George] The victim fell backward through the curtain into the private room, where Mrs.

79
00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:59.920
[George] Bender or Kate swiftly slit the victim's throat with a butcher knife to ensure death.

80
00:09:00.280 --> 00:09:13.400
[George] The trapdoor was thrown open, and the corpse was dropped into the dark cellar pit below until nightfall, when the body was stripped of currency, watches and clothes, and buried in the apple orchard under cover of darkness.

81
00:09:13.400 --> 00:09:22.440
[George] In total, the Benders had netted only a few thousand dollars in cash, some horses, and pocket watches for twelve cold-blooded murders.

82
00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:32.440
[George] The cellar trapdoor beneath the dining table was soaked in dried blood, revealing the mechanical slaughter trap where diners were hammered from behind through the canvas curtain.

83
00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:38.680
[George] The discovery of the Bender Boneyard ignited a nationwide sensation and a massive manhunt.

84
00:09:38.680 --> 00:09:46.440
[George] Kansas Governor Thomas Osborne offered a massive reward of $3,000, while the York family offered thousands more.

85
00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:53.560
[George] Posses of Pinkerton detectives, Texas Rangers and vigilantes scoured the West from Texas to California.

86
00:09:53.560 --> 00:10:01.880
[George] The Bender's abandoned wagon and starving horses were found near Thayer, Kansas, where the family had purchased train tickets heading south.

87
00:10:01.880 --> 00:10:15.080
[George] Yet, despite dozens of reported sightings, arrests of suspected women across Colorado and Michigan, and persistent rumors that an armed vigilante posse had caught and lynched the entire family in the Indian territory.

88
00:10:15.080 --> 00:10:20.680
[George] Not a single member of the Bender family was ever definitively captured or brought to trial.

89
00:10:20.680 --> 00:10:34.760
[George] The bloody Benders vanished into the mists of Western folklore, leaving behind a ruined cabin that was torn to pieces by souvenir hunters, and a macabre legend that earned them the grim title of America's first serial killer family.

90
00:10:35.240 --> 00:10:40.840
[Rebecca] The macabre tale of the Bloody Benders reminds us that the frontier held darkness as well as light.

91
00:10:40.840 --> 00:10:48.160
[Rebecca] It shows that evil can wear the mask of hospitality, and that some mysteries of the West remain buried in the prairie earth forever.

92
00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:51.120
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

93
00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:52.320
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

94
